---
# IS ANY JVM ABOUT TO RUN OUT OF HEAP.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FAILURE THIS EXISTS FOR
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# web-us OOMed on 2026-09-03T14:53 with `-Xmx768m` while a commit raising it to 1024m had been
# merged, green and tagged `deployed-web` for nearly three hours. NOTHING WAS RED. The CI deploy
# endpoint can only run `kubectl set image` (infra/nix/modules/roles/k8s-deploy.nix -- it "cannot
# run kubectl, and cannot name a resource"), so the new IMAGE rolled and the ConfigMap carrying
# JAVA_OPTS never moved. The tier ran for hours at 88% of a heap nobody believed it still had, and
# the first sign was a 502.
#
# So the rule below is deliberately NOT "did the config apply". Config drift is one way to arrive
# here; a corpus that outgrew its heap is another, and `web-uk` and `web-pl` are on that path today
# at 94.5% and 87.5% of their own caps. What both have in common is the only thing worth alerting
# on: A JVM WHOSE LIVE SET NO LONGER LEAVES ROOM TO COLLECT.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY OLD GEN, AND WHY NOT THE HEAP PEAK
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Peak heap ~= -Xmx is NORMAL -- that is when a collection runs, and every healthy pod here reads
# 93-99%. Alerting on it pages for health. The live set is the signal, and its proxy is the OLD
# GENERATION: tenured data that survived collection.
#
# `min_over_time(...[30m])` rather than the instantaneous value, because old-gen occupancy
# sawtooths -- it climbs with tenured garbage and drops when a mixed collection reclaims it. The
# floor of that sawtooth is what cannot be collected away, and it is the number that decides
# whether the next large allocation has anywhere to go. On web-us the floor walked 630 -> 725 MiB
# in the two hours before the OOM while the instantaneous value looked merely busy.
#
# ⚠️ THE FLOOR IS NOT THE LIVE SET, and this rule does not claim it is. G1 only truly compacts at a
# full collection, so the floor drifts upward under a stable live set too -- measured on this very
# incident, two forced full GCs 13 minutes apart showed the live set FLAT while the floor climbed.
# That is not a defect in the alert: a floor that has climbed past the threshold means the room
# left for allocation is gone whether the cause is retention or deferred compaction, and both end
# in the same OOM.
#
# ⚠️ ON THE WORKERS (SerialGC until 2026-10-24, Parallel since: both reclaim tenured only in a full
# collection) THE FLOOR IS WEAKER STILL, and reading it as retention sent the
# 2026-09-05 worker-pl investigation off in the wrong direction for a while. G1 marks concurrently
# and collects often, so its floor tracks the live set loosely but continuously. Serial and Parallel only ever
# reclaims tenured at a FULL collection, and these workers do very few -- worker-pl did 2 in 24h
# while healthy. Between two full GCs the floor is simply "everything tenured since the last one",
# which is garbage and live data indistinguishable, so a floor that has climbed says nothing about
# retention until a full GC has actually run under it. Before believing a worker's floor, check
# `increase(jvm_gc_collection_seconds_count{gc="PS MarkSweep"}[30m])` (`MarkSweepCompact` under
# SerialGC) over the same window.
#
# The tell that a worker is genuinely out of room is not the floor at all: it is the young
# collector (`PS Scavenge`; Serial's `Copy`) FROZEN while the full one (`PS MarkSweep`; Serial's
# `MarkSweepCompact`) climbs. Both collectors skip a young collection for a full one once tenured
# cannot guarantee promotion, so a flat young-GC counter means every collection is already a full
# one -- worker-pl (SerialGC) sat at exactly 313.2 MB of a 313.2 MB pool doing ~2 full GCs a minute
# and reclaiming nothing.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# "THE CORPUS OUTGREW THE HEAP" IS A GUESS, AND IT WAS WRONG ONCE ALREADY
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# worker-pl fired this alert on 2026-09-05 at 99.69% while carrying the SMALLEST showtime corpus in
# the fleet -- 49k against worker-uk's 187k, which sat at 57% on an identical 448m heap. Nothing
# had outgrown anything. A cache of whole HTML detail pages was bounded by ENTRY COUNT
# (`maximumSize(10000)`), so 1,015 entries held 228 MiB of a 313 MiB old gen while nine thousand
# entries short of its bound. The fix was the cache, not the heap.
#
# So: measure what is retained before resizing anything. These pods are JRE images with no
# `jcmd`/`jmap`, and the attach procedure that gets a histogram or an hprof out of one anyway is in
# the runbook -- take it BEFORE restarting the pod, because a restart is also the thing that
# destroys the evidence.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE POOL NAMES DIFFER BY COLLECTOR, WHICH IS WHY THE MATCH IS A REGEX
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# web runs G1 (`-XX:+UseG1GC`) and its tenured pool is `G1 Old Gen`; the workers run the Parallel
# collector (`PS Old Gen`, `PS MarkSweep`), SerialGC (`Tenured Gen`, `MarkSweepCompact`) until 2026-10-24. A rule naming only one silently covers half the fleet -- the exact shape
# k3s.rules warns about, where a series that never exists reads as coverage.
#
# ⚠️ HISTORY STARTS AT THE 2026-08-29 CUTOVER. `for:` windows longer than that were meaningless
# before it and are fine now, but do not reach further back when tuning these.
groups:
  - name: jvm-heap
    rules:
      - alert: JvmOldGenNearCap
        # The floor of the sawtooth, as a fraction of the pool's own cap -- on a Serial/Parallel worker only
        # once a full collection has run under it, because before one its floor is everything tenured
        # since the last, garbage included (the header above). worker-es paged on 2026-10-18 at 0.89
        # with no MarkSweepCompact in 2.5h; the first one, at 11:40Z, dropped it to 0.60. worker-de's
        # real thrash on 10-12 ran 40-600 full GCs per half hour under a 0.88-0.99 floor and still pages.
        expr: >-
          min_over_time(jvm_memory_pool_used_bytes{pool="G1 Old Gen"}[30m])
            / on (instance, pool) group_left
          jvm_memory_pool_max_bytes{pool="G1 Old Gen"} > 0.85
          or
          (
            min_over_time(jvm_memory_pool_used_bytes{pool=~"Tenured Gen|PS Old Gen"}[30m])
              / on (instance, pool) group_left
            jvm_memory_pool_max_bytes{pool=~"Tenured Gen|PS Old Gen"} > 0.85
          )
          and on (instance)
          increase(jvm_gc_collection_seconds_count{gc=~"MarkSweepCompact|PS MarkSweep"}[30m]) > 0
        # TWO HOURS, because this is a trend and not an event. web-us took ~2h from restart to OOM,
        # so this fires with time to act on the pod that is going to die -- and a deploy rolling a
        # pod resets the measurement, which is what keeps a routine restart from paging.
        for: 2h
        labels:
          severity: warning
          domain: platform
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.job }} {{ $labels.country }} old gen is {{ $value | humanizePercentage }} of its cap"
          description: >-
            The tenured floor has sat above 85% of this JVM's heap cap for two hours, so what is
            left is not enough to collect into and the next large allocation is the one that
            fails. This is the shape that OOM-killed web-us on 2026-09-03.
            FIRST CHECK THAT THE HEAP IS THE SIZE THE REPOSITORY THINKS IT IS -- Flux reconciles the ConfigMap now and Reloader rolls the pod on a real change, so a
            disagreement here means Flux is FAILING rather than merely waiting -- see flux.rules. Compare
            `kubectl -n kinowo get cm <tier>-env-<country> -o jsonpath='{.data.JAVA_OPTS}'`
            against the overlay. If they already agree, something RETAINS more than this heap
            holds -- which is NOT always the corpus, so measure before resizing. On a worker
            (Parallel GC) this pages only while full collections run under the floor, so it is the
            live set, not uncollected garbage. worker-pl paged here on 2026-09-05 carrying the fleet's SMALLEST corpus, because a
            cache bounded by entry count held 228 MiB of HTML; the fix was the cache, not the heap.

      # IS A JVM'S WHOLE PROCESS ABOUT TO OUTGROW ITS CONTAINER -- the failure the rule above
      # CANNOT see.
      #
      # `-XX:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError` turns heap exhaustion into a clean exit, so a container that
      # is OOM-KILLED grew past its cgroup limit OUTSIDE the heap: metaspace, code cache, direct
      # buffers, thread stacks, GC structures, and -- the one that has actually happened -- malloc'd
      # memory no JVM pool reports at all. web-pl was killed seven times on 2026-09-21 17:01-18:25Z
      # while AhrefsBot swept the film share cards: every progressive-JPEG poster decode holds a
      # native buffer the size of the whole image, RSS climbed to ~0.87 of the 1 GiB limit, and 445 MB
      # of it belonged to no pool. Heap stayed under -Xmx the whole time, so JvmOldGenNearCap was
      # silent, and the first signal was K3sContainerOOMKilled -- after the fact.
      #
      # RSS AGAINST THE POD'S OWN LIMIT, read from kube-state-metrics so an overlay that resizes a
      # limit moves the threshold with it. The app series carry `tier`/`country` from the scrape
      # config; the limit series carry `container` (= tier) and a pod name that holds the country,
      # so both are lifted into labels to join on. `max by` because a rolling deploy briefly has two
      # pods (and two limit series) per country.
      #
      # A CLIMB, NOT A LEVEL. The first version fired at a static 0.82 (web) / 0.92 (worker), and
      # no static line separates the two shapes prod has shown (replayed 2026-09-24): web-pl sat
      # FLAT at 0.86-0.87 for ten hours on 09-20 and again on 09-21 with no kill, while the one
      # 09-21 kill that grew rather than jumped climbed 0.61 -> 0.89 in the half hour before it.
      # So this asks whether RSS, on its last 30 minutes' straight line, reaches the limit within
      # the next 30, AND is already over 0.75 of it -- the floor keeps a boot filling its caches
      # quiet. Over the seven days to 09-24 that fired on web-pl's 09-17 crash loop and five
      # minutes before 09-21's 18:25Z kill, and on nothing else in either tier; since the web pods
      # got MALLOC_ARENA_MAX=2 (09-24 04:34Z) they sit at 0.54-0.67. A single decode storm can
      # still go from quiet to killed between two 30s scrapes (most of 09-21's seven kills did);
      # nothing sampled can promise to see that one. The series survives a pod restart (the
      # target is the country's NodePort), so a restart's drop pulls the line down, not up.
      - alert: JvmResidentNearContainerLimit
        expr: >-
          (
            max by (tier, country) (process_resident_memory_bytes{job=~"kinowo-web|kinowo-worker"})
              / on (tier, country)
            max by (tier, country) (
              label_replace(
                label_replace(kube_pod_container_resource_limits{namespace="kinowo", resource="memory", container=~"web|worker"},
                  "tier", "$1", "container", "(.*)"),
                "country", "$1", "pod", "(?:web|worker)-([a-z]+)-.*")
            )
          ) > 0.75
          and on (tier, country)
          (
            max by (tier, country) (predict_linear(process_resident_memory_bytes{job=~"kinowo-web|kinowo-worker"}[30m], 1800))
              >= on (tier, country)
            max by (tier, country) (
              label_replace(
                label_replace(kube_pod_container_resource_limits{namespace="kinowo", resource="memory", container=~"web|worker"},
                  "tier", "$1", "container", "(.*)"),
                "country", "$1", "pod", "(?:web|worker)-([a-z]+)-.*")
            )
          )
        for: 2m
        labels:
          severity: warning
          domain: platform
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.tier }}-{{ $labels.country }} RSS is {{ $value | humanizePercentage }} of its container memory limit and climbing toward it"
          description: >-
            The process's resident memory is climbing toward the cgroup limit fast enough to reach
            it within half an hour, and the kernel -- not the JVM -- decides what happens then: an OOM kill with no heap dump and no log line. Find WHICH memory grew
            before resizing anything. Heap, non-heap pools and direct buffers are
            `jvm_memory_committed_bytes` and `jvm_buffer_pool_used_bytes{pool="direct"}`; RSS minus
            their sum is memory NO JVM POOL SEES (native allocations: image decoding, fonts, glibc
            arenas). On web that used to be the og-image share cards under a crawler sweep; the worker
            renders them since 2026-09-24, and its poster shrinks run in a vips subprocess that counts
            against the same cgroup -- check `kinowo_worker_share_cards_render_total` for a render
            burst. Raising the limit only buys time if the growth is unbounded.

      # THE STRING DEDUPLICATION TABLE IS NATIVE MEMORY WITH NO CAP. `-XX:+UseStringDeduplication`
      # (infra/jvm/worker.options) keeps its table OUTSIDE -Xmx, and it grows with what the collector
      # visits: a heap thrashing at its cap visits everything, over and over. On 2026-10-02 22:20Z a
      # change that held every unmatched listing's scored candidates (3f4bf656a, fixed by
      # 161fbc450) pinned the uk and de heaps at their caps; the table grew 55 MB -> 627 MB on uk,
      # ~550 MB on de and us, and the memory-cgroup kills that followed crash-looped four workers
      # for four and a half hours. JvmResidentNearContainerLimit saw it only on de and pl -- most
      # boots went from quiet to killed inside its 30-minute line -- and HeapDumpWritten only
      # after the fact. This table was the one number that climbed on EVERY worker, minutes ahead.
      #
      # 150 MB, HELD FIVE MINUTES. Measured 2026-10-05 over the week: p99 per country 5-30 MB, the
      # largest outside the storm ~50 MB, and 300-765 MB inside it (worker-us reached 518 MB in
      # its 09-30 kills too). `last_over_time` bridges a restart, which re-seeds the table at 0
      # and clears this honestly.
      - alert: JvmStringDedupTableLarge
        expr: >-
          max by (country) (last_over_time(kinowo_worker_native_memory_bytes{category="String Deduplication"}[10m]))
            > 150 * 1024 * 1024
        for: 5m
        labels:
          severity: warning
          domain: platform
        annotations:
          summary: "worker-{{ $labels.country }}'s String Deduplication table is {{ $value | humanize1024 }}B of native memory"
          description: >-
            The JVM's String Deduplication table lives outside -Xmx with no cap, and it grows when
            the collector keeps visiting a full heap -- so this is a heap thrashing at its cap,
            spending native memory the pod's limit does not budget for. The cgroup OOM kill is
            usually minutes away. Look at the heap first: `jvm_memory_pool_used_bytes` for the
            tenured pool against its max, and the VITALS lines in the worker's log ("String
            Deduplication=" among the top native categories). The 2026-10-02 storm was a change
            that retained a scored candidate set per unmatched listing (3f4bf656a). Raising the
            limit only buys time: find what fills the heap.

      # HOW OFTEN A JVM RUNS A FULL (OLD-GENERATION) COLLECTION, per pod, on both tiers and whatever
      # the collector. A full collection stops the whole JVM for as long as it takes to walk the live
      # set (SerialGC on worker-us: ~3.5 s over ~450 MB), and a JVM that runs them back to back is
      # one allocation away from OutOfMemoryError -- or, as on worker-us at 2026-10-24 06:02Z, from
      # the cgroup's OOM kill: its tenured pool sat at 768 of 768 MiB after every one of ~80 full
      # collections in nine minutes, the String Deduplication table grew in native memory beside it,
      # and the kernel killed the pod at 06:11:46Z. JvmOldGenNearCap holds two hours; this is the
      # event, minutes after it starts.
      #
      # THE COLLECTOR NAMES ARE EACH COLLECTOR'S OLD-GENERATION BEAN: `MarkSweepCompact` (Serial),
      # `PS MarkSweep` (Parallel), `G1 Old Generation` (a G1 FULL collection -- its mixed ones count
      # as young), `ZGC Major Cycles` / `ZGC Cycles` (ZGC). Prometheus held `Copy`,
      # `MarkSweepCompact`, `G1 Young Generation`, `G1 Old Generation` and `G1 Concurrent GC` on
      # 2026-10-24. Shenandoah has no old-generation bean of its own, so it is not covered.
      #
      # 60 AN HOUR -- ONE A MINUTE -- READ OVER 30 MINUTES, HELD 10. Measured over the 7 days to
      # 2026-10-24: a steady worker ran 1-9 an hour (median per country), a boot ~9 in its first 20
      # minutes (DE: 404 full collections over ~45 boots), web's G1 none in most hours (worst hour 9,
      # web-de). Backtested over the 14 days to 10-24: at 30 an hour the SerialGC workers fired 33
      # times (16.5 a week: every hourly whole-corpus projection that met a nearly full tenured pool);
      # at 60 about 10 raw episodes, the thrashes this is for -- 80 in 9 minutes on worker-us 10-24
      # 06:02Z, 40-600 a half hour on worker-de 10-11/12. The 30-minute `increase` bridges a worker
      # restart's ~3-minute gap.
      - alert: JvmFullGcRateHigh
        expr: >-
          sum by (job, country) (
            increase(jvm_gc_collection_seconds_count{job=~"kinowo-web|kinowo-worker",
              gc=~"MarkSweepCompact|PS MarkSweep|G1 Old Generation|ZGC Major Cycles|ZGC Cycles"}[30m])
          ) * 2 > 60
        for: 10m
        labels:
          severity: warning
          domain: platform
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.job }} {{ $labels.country }} runs {{ $value | humanize }} full GCs an hour"
          description: >-
            This JVM is running full (old-generation) collections back to back: each one stops it
            for seconds, and a run of them means the old generation has almost nothing left to
            reclaim. Read `jvm_memory_pool_collection_used_bytes` for its old-gen pool (Tenured Gen,
            PS Old Gen, G1 Old Gen) against `jvm_memory_pool_max_bytes`: near 1.0 after every
            collection is a live set that no longer fits, so take a class histogram NOW (`kubectl
            -n kinowo exec deploy/<tier>-<country> -- jcmd 1 GC.class_histogram`) before the pod
            is killed and the evidence with it. Watch JvmStringDedupTableLarge and
            JvmResidentNearContainerLimit beside it: the native memory a thrash grows is what gets
            a worker OOM-killed. Raising -Xmx only buys time if something retains more than it should.

      # HOW MUCH OF THE WALL CLOCK A JVM SPENDS STOPPED FOR COLLECTIONS. `jvm_gc_collection_seconds`
      # is each collector bean's accumulated collection time; for every stop-the-world bean that is
      # pause time, so their summed rate is the share of the wall clock the JVM was paused. The
      # CONCURRENT beans (`G1 Concurrent GC`, ZGC's and Shenandoah's `* Cycles`) time work done
      # BESIDE the application -- a G1 marking cycle runs for seconds without stopping anything --
      # and are left out, or web would read as paused while it served.
      #
      # 10%, OVER 30 MINUTES, HELD 30. Measured over the 7 days to 2026-10-24 (30-minute windows):
      # web p99 0.0-0.5% (max 0.7%); workers p99 0.4-5.8%, max 13.8% (de), 20.3% (uk), 29.2% (us) --
      # those maxima the boots and heap thrashes this is for. A boot alone spends ~20 s in full
      # collections, about 1% of half an hour.
      - alert: JvmGcTimeShareHigh
        expr: >-
          sum by (job, country) (
            rate(jvm_gc_collection_seconds_sum{job=~"kinowo-web|kinowo-worker",
              gc!~"G1 Concurrent GC|ZGC Major Cycles|ZGC Minor Cycles|ZGC Cycles|Shenandoah Cycles"}[30m])
          ) > 0.10
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
          domain: platform
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.job }} {{ $labels.country }} spends {{ $value | humanizePercentage }} of its wall clock paused for GC"
          description: >-
            For half an hour this JVM has been stopped for garbage collection more than a tenth of
            the time: its scrapes, projections or requests wait out every pause. Split it by `gc`
            (`rate(jvm_gc_collection_seconds_sum[30m])`): mostly the old-generation collector is a
            heap too full to collect into (see JvmFullGcRateHigh and the old-gen pool after GC);
            mostly the young one is an allocation rate the young generation is too small for --
            `jvm_memory_pool_allocated_bytes_total` for the eden pool says which.

      # THE OLD GENERATION AFTER A COLLECTION, AGAINST ITS CAP: the leading signal of a full-GC
      # thrash, read where the collector itself last measured the live set. Unlike
      # JvmOldGenNearCap's floor of the used sawtooth (two hours, a trend), this is the value each
      # collection leaves (`jvm_memory_pool_collection_used_bytes`), so it moves the moment a live
      # set grows past what the generation holds.
      #
      # TWO BRANCHES, BECAUSE THE TWO COLLECTOR FAMILIES WRITE IT DIFFERENTLY. G1 rewrites it after
      # every young and mixed collection, so a G1 old gen above 90% after collections for half an
      # hour is full. Serial and Parallel write it only after a FULL collection and then hold it,
      # so a value one heavy tick left could sit there for hours with nothing wrong: that branch
      # counts only while full collections run (the same gate as JvmOldGenNearCap).
      #
      # 90%, HELD 30 MINUTES. Measured over the 7 days to 2026-10-24 (per pod, after collections):
      # web max 0.31-0.96 (web-de 0.96, web-us 0.91: single G1 collections inside a burst, under the
      # hold); workers 0.86-0.93 outside a thrash, and 1.0 in worker-us's (10-24 06:02-06:11Z).
      # `last_over_time` carries a worker's gauge across a restart's gap.
      - alert: JvmOldGenAfterGcNearCap
        expr: >-
          (
            max by (job, country, pool) (last_over_time(jvm_memory_pool_collection_used_bytes{job=~"kinowo-web|kinowo-worker", pool=~"G1 Old Gen|ZGC Old Generation"}[10m]))
              / on (job, country, pool)
            max by (job, country, pool) (last_over_time(jvm_memory_pool_max_bytes{job=~"kinowo-web|kinowo-worker", pool=~"G1 Old Gen|ZGC Old Generation"}[10m]))
          ) > 0.90
          or
          (
            (
              max by (job, country, pool) (last_over_time(jvm_memory_pool_collection_used_bytes{job=~"kinowo-web|kinowo-worker", pool=~"Tenured Gen|PS Old Gen"}[10m]))
                / on (job, country, pool)
              max by (job, country, pool) (last_over_time(jvm_memory_pool_max_bytes{job=~"kinowo-web|kinowo-worker", pool=~"Tenured Gen|PS Old Gen"}[10m]))
            ) > 0.90
            and on (job, country)
            sum by (job, country) (increase(jvm_gc_collection_seconds_count{job=~"kinowo-web|kinowo-worker", gc=~"MarkSweepCompact|PS MarkSweep"}[30m])) > 0
          )
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
          domain: platform
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.job }} {{ $labels.country }} {{ $labels.pool }} is {{ $value | humanizePercentage }} full after collections"
          description: >-
            What the collector leaves in the old generation has stayed above 90% of its cap for
            half an hour: the next allocation burst has almost nowhere to go, and full collections
            that reclaim nothing come next (JvmFullGcRateHigh). Take a class histogram before
            anything restarts the pod (`jcmd 1 GC.class_histogram`), and compare the live set with
            NodeMemoryBudgetSpec's MeasuredLiveSetMaxMib for the country: a live set that grew with
            the corpus wants the heap re-sized there; one that jumped wants the structure found.

      # IS A JVM'S BASELINE MEMORY HIGHER THAN IT WAS A WEEK AGO -- the slow regression every rule
      # above is blind to. Each of them measures against a CAP (or a 30-minute straight line toward
      # one), so a live set that grows 40% and still fits stays silent until the day it does not.
      # Nothing noticed an identity change that raised per-tick allocation 5.1 -> 9.4 MB, and nothing
      # would notice a cache that creeps up for a fortnight. This compares each JVM with ITSELF, same
      # weekday, seven days earlier, so a change is caught in the week it ships.
      #
      # THE 24-HOUR MEDIAN, NOT A SHORT FLOOR. The first draft was `min_over_time(...[6h])`, and
      # replayed over the 14 days to 2026-10-24 it held 49 episodes, because a boot's collections
      # leave the old gen near empty and a SerialGC/Parallel worker reports 0 until its first full
      # collection: one restart in the week-ago window divides by almost nothing (the ratio read
      # `inf` on every worker). The median ignores a boot, covers a whole daily traffic cycle, and
      # moves only once a change has held for half a day -- the right speed for a baseline.
      #
      # THE OLD GEN AFTER COLLECTION (`jvm_memory_pool_collection_used_bytes`), one series per JVM:
      # `max by (job, country)` drops the pool label so the workers' 2026-10-24 switch from SerialGC
      # (`Tenured Gen`) to Parallel (`PS Old Gen`) compares like with like instead of reading as a
      # brand-new JVM for a week (431 vs 454 MiB on worker-de across it). On G1 (web) it includes
      # garbage awaiting a mixed collection, so web's daily medians swing 20-40% on their own.
      #
      # IT FIRES ON THE CROSSING, NOT FOR AS LONG AS THE GAP LASTS. A week-over-week comparison stays
      # true for up to seven days after a real step -- until the step enters the week-ago window --
      # so the plain level fired 50-68% of the replayed fortnight and repeated a Telegram warning
      # every 12 hours for a week about one change. The `unless` arm drops a JVM that already met
      # the condition four hours ago: each crossing fires once (held an hour, so for about three)
      # and resolves. One notice per regression is the point; the graph is where to watch it after.
      #
      # 1.25x AND +128 MiB. Daily medians over 2026-10-03..24: workers move < 10% a week except at
      # real steps -- worker-de 263 -> 400 MiB on 10-13 (caught) and worker-uk 285 -> 353 on 10-14
      # (MISSED at this floor: +96 MiB caught it but, with web's G1 swings, fired 8 times in 14 days
      # and blew the backtest budget). Replayed, this fired 3 times: worker-de, and web-us's
      # 349 -> 512 MiB climb 10-17..20, which the whole web tier shared and gave back on 10-21.
      # The absolute floor keeps a 70 MiB heap doubling quiet.
      #
      # NO WEEK-AGO DATA, NO ALERT. The comparison drops a series whose week-ago window is empty, and
      # the `offset 8d` presence term also requires the JVM to have existed when that window OPENED,
      # so a country added 6 days ago does not compare its first day's boot against today. A new
      # country is therefore unwatched by this rule for its first eight days.
      - alert: JvmLiveHeapBaselineRising
        expr: >-
          (
            quantile_over_time(0.5, (max by (job, country) (jvm_memory_pool_collection_used_bytes{job=~"kinowo-web|kinowo-worker", pool=~"G1 Old Gen|Tenured Gen|PS Old Gen"}))[24h:5m])
              > 1.25 * quantile_over_time(0.5, (max by (job, country) (jvm_memory_pool_collection_used_bytes{job=~"kinowo-web|kinowo-worker", pool=~"G1 Old Gen|Tenured Gen|PS Old Gen"}))[24h:5m] offset 7d)
            and
            quantile_over_time(0.5, (max by (job, country) (jvm_memory_pool_collection_used_bytes{job=~"kinowo-web|kinowo-worker", pool=~"G1 Old Gen|Tenured Gen|PS Old Gen"}))[24h:5m])
              - quantile_over_time(0.5, (max by (job, country) (jvm_memory_pool_collection_used_bytes{job=~"kinowo-web|kinowo-worker", pool=~"G1 Old Gen|Tenured Gen|PS Old Gen"}))[24h:5m] offset 7d)
              > 128 * 1024 * 1024
            and on (job, country)
            max by (job, country) (last_over_time(jvm_memory_pool_collection_used_bytes{job=~"kinowo-web|kinowo-worker", pool=~"G1 Old Gen|Tenured Gen|PS Old Gen"}[1h] offset 8d))
          )
          unless on (job, country)
          (
            quantile_over_time(0.5, (max by (job, country) (jvm_memory_pool_collection_used_bytes{job=~"kinowo-web|kinowo-worker", pool=~"G1 Old Gen|Tenured Gen|PS Old Gen"}))[24h:5m] offset 4h)
              > 1.25 * quantile_over_time(0.5, (max by (job, country) (jvm_memory_pool_collection_used_bytes{job=~"kinowo-web|kinowo-worker", pool=~"G1 Old Gen|Tenured Gen|PS Old Gen"}))[24h:5m] offset 7d4h)
            and
            quantile_over_time(0.5, (max by (job, country) (jvm_memory_pool_collection_used_bytes{job=~"kinowo-web|kinowo-worker", pool=~"G1 Old Gen|Tenured Gen|PS Old Gen"}))[24h:5m] offset 4h)
              - quantile_over_time(0.5, (max by (job, country) (jvm_memory_pool_collection_used_bytes{job=~"kinowo-web|kinowo-worker", pool=~"G1 Old Gen|Tenured Gen|PS Old Gen"}))[24h:5m] offset 7d4h)
              > 128 * 1024 * 1024
          )
        for: 1h
        labels:
          severity: warning
          domain: platform
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.job }} {{ $labels.country }} old gen after GC is {{ $value | humanize1024 }}B (24h median), up from {{ with printf `quantile_over_time(0.5, (max by (job, country) (jvm_memory_pool_collection_used_bytes{job=%q, country=%q, pool=~\"G1 Old Gen|Tenured Gen|PS Old Gen\"}))[24h:5m] offset 7d)` $labels.job $labels.country | query }}{{ . | first | value | humanize1024 }}B{{ end }} a week ago"
          description: >-
            What collections leave in this JVM's old generation -- its live set plus what is
            waiting to be collected -- has just risen more than 25% (and 128 MiB) above the same
            day last week. Nothing is near a cap yet; this is the regression that gets there in a
            few weeks, announced once and resolved after a few hours, not repeated. Compare the
            commits deployed this week (`deployed-*` tags), then find what retains it: a class
            histogram (`jcmd 1 GC.class_histogram`) or a heap dump (docs/heap-dumps.md), and the
            `worker-diagnostics` dashboard. A per-tick allocation change shows in the allocation
            budgets in tools.costs.PerformanceBudgets. A planned corpus or heap change raises this
            too.

      # THE SAME QUESTION FOR THE WHOLE PROCESS: resident memory, which on top of the heap counts
      # metaspace, code cache, thread stacks, direct buffers, the String Deduplication table and
      # malloc'd memory no JVM pool reports (the 445 MB that OOM-killed web-pl on 2026-09-21). A
      # native leak never shows in the old gen, so the rule above cannot see it.
      #
      # RSS IS STEADIER THAN THE OLD GEN -- a JVM commits its heap and keeps it -- so the same shape
      # carries less noise: web's daily medians stayed within ~10% for three weeks. 1.25x AND
      # +128 MiB, fired on the crossing like the rule above: replayed over the 14 days to 2026-10-24
      # it fired on worker-de's 10-13 step (1009 -> 1213 MiB, alongside its old gen) and three times
      # on worker-es climbing ~600 -> ~780 MiB over 10-07..10, and on nothing else. 1.15x added
      # web-es, worker-pl and worker-uk at 15-20%. Same week-ago and presence guards as above.
      - alert: ProcessResidentBaselineRising
        expr: >-
          (
            quantile_over_time(0.5, (max by (job, country) (process_resident_memory_bytes{job=~"kinowo-web|kinowo-worker"}))[24h:5m])
              > 1.25 * quantile_over_time(0.5, (max by (job, country) (process_resident_memory_bytes{job=~"kinowo-web|kinowo-worker"}))[24h:5m] offset 7d)
            and
            quantile_over_time(0.5, (max by (job, country) (process_resident_memory_bytes{job=~"kinowo-web|kinowo-worker"}))[24h:5m])
              - quantile_over_time(0.5, (max by (job, country) (process_resident_memory_bytes{job=~"kinowo-web|kinowo-worker"}))[24h:5m] offset 7d)
              > 128 * 1024 * 1024
            and on (job, country)
            max by (job, country) (last_over_time(process_resident_memory_bytes{job=~"kinowo-web|kinowo-worker"}[1h] offset 8d))
          )
          unless on (job, country)
          (
            quantile_over_time(0.5, (max by (job, country) (process_resident_memory_bytes{job=~"kinowo-web|kinowo-worker"}))[24h:5m] offset 4h)
              > 1.25 * quantile_over_time(0.5, (max by (job, country) (process_resident_memory_bytes{job=~"kinowo-web|kinowo-worker"}))[24h:5m] offset 7d4h)
            and
            quantile_over_time(0.5, (max by (job, country) (process_resident_memory_bytes{job=~"kinowo-web|kinowo-worker"}))[24h:5m] offset 4h)
              - quantile_over_time(0.5, (max by (job, country) (process_resident_memory_bytes{job=~"kinowo-web|kinowo-worker"}))[24h:5m] offset 7d4h)
              > 128 * 1024 * 1024
          )
        for: 1h
        labels:
          severity: warning
          domain: platform
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.job }} {{ $labels.country }} resident memory is {{ $value | humanize1024 }}B (24h median), up from {{ with printf `quantile_over_time(0.5, (max by (job, country) (process_resident_memory_bytes{job=%q, country=%q}))[24h:5m] offset 7d)` $labels.job $labels.country | query }}{{ . | first | value | humanize1024 }}B{{ end }} a week ago"
          description: >-
            This JVM's resident memory has just risen more than 25% (and 128 MiB) above the same
            day last week, so its next spike starts that much closer to the container limit
            (JvmResidentNearContainerLimit); announced once, resolved after a few hours. Split it
            first: heap and non-heap pools are `jvm_memory_committed_bytes`, direct buffers
            `jvm_buffer_pool_used_bytes{pool="direct"}`, the worker's native categories
            `kinowo_worker_native_memory_bytes`; RSS minus those is memory no JVM pool sees. Then
            compare the commits deployed this week (`deployed-*` tags), take a heap dump
            (docs/heap-dumps.md) or read the `worker-diagnostics` dashboard, and check the
            allocation budgets in tools.costs.PerformanceBudgets. A planned heap or limit change
            raises this too.

      # A JVM WROTE A HEAP DUMP, i.e. it died of (or was about to die of) heap exhaustion and left
      # the evidence on k3s-worker-1. `ExitOnOutOfMemoryError` makes a heap OOM a clean exit and a
      # quiet restart, so without this the dump is the only trace -- and the budget keeps just the
      # 3 newest per app-country, so a crash loop rotates the first, most telling one away.
      #
      # THE NEWEST WRITE TIME MOVED FORWARD, not "a dump exists": the timer republishes the same
      # value every 15 minutes, so existence would page for ever. It only ever increases (the prune
      # deletes oldest first, and compression keeps the mtime). 0 means an empty directory, so the
      # first dump in a directory is 0 -> T and fires like any other. The 30m window spans two
      # timer runs, so one missed scrape cannot swallow it; the rule resolves by itself once the
      # value has been flat for that long, which is right for an event.
      - alert: HeapDumpWritten
        expr: >-
          kinowo_heapdumps_newest_timestamp_seconds
            > (kinowo_heapdumps_newest_timestamp_seconds offset 30m)
        labels:
          severity: warning
          domain: platform
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.dir }} wrote a heap dump on {{ $labels.host }}"
          description: >-
            A {{ $labels.dir }} JVM wrote a heap dump -- a heap OOM (or the worker's liveness
            watchdog catching a wedged heap), which restarts the container quietly. The dump is in
            /var/lib/kinowo/heapdumps/{{ $labels.dir }}/ on k3s-worker-1 and only the 3 newest per
            directory are kept, so COPY IT OFF FIRST: see docs/heap-dumps.md for scp and reading
            it. `kinowo_heapdumps_files` and `kinowo_heapdumps_bytes` say what is held.

      # THE TIMER HeapDumpWritten STANDS ON. That rule, and the node-wide budget, both come from
      # the kinowo-heap-dumps timer (modules/fleet/heap-dumps.nix), so a timer that stops leaves a
      # dump that lands unannounced and a hostPath bounded only by each container's boot prune --
      # the same "the collector stopped" shape ContainerImageGarbageCollectionStale watches for
      # the image store, on a 15-minute timer instead of a daily one. TWO HOURS is eight missed
      # runs (a run may take up to its 30-minute TimeoutStartSec after a crash loop), so a slow
      # pass or a node reboot does not page.
      - alert: HeapDumpBudgetStale
        expr: time() - kinowo_heapdumps_prune_last_success_timestamp_seconds > 2 * 3600
        labels:
          severity: warning
          domain: platform
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.host }} has not pruned or reported its heap dumps for over 2 hours"
          description: >-
            The kinowo-heap-dumps timer holds the node's heap dumps to their budget and publishes
            the `kinowo_heapdumps_*` series HeapDumpWritten reads, so while it is stopped a new
            dump neither alerts nor is bounded by anything but each container's own boot prune.
            `systemctl status kinowo-heap-dumps.service kinowo-heap-dumps.timer` and
            `journalctl -u kinowo-heap-dumps` on the node; the script is
            infra/nix/files/heap-dumps.sh.

      # ...AND THE NODE THAT NEVER PUBLISHED IT. The rule above is a comparison and cannot fire on
      # a series that is not there -- which is exactly the state a new unit leaves the node in
      # until somebody switches it in by hand, since auto-apply refuses the switch that first
      # introduces a unit. Joined on `role="k3s-worker"`, the node every kinowo pod is pinned to
      # and the only one with `fleet.heapDumps.enable`: `k3s.service` would not do, monitoring-1
      # runs the k3s SERVER and hosts no kinowo pod. An hour covers the first run plus its
      # randomised delay.
      - alert: HeapDumpBudgetMetricAbsent
        expr: >-
          up{job="node",role="k3s-worker"} == 1
            unless on (host) kinowo_heapdumps_prune_last_success_timestamp_seconds
        for: 1h
        labels:
          severity: warning
          domain: platform
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.host }} has never reported its heap-dump budget"
          description: >-
            HeapDumpBudgetStale and HeapDumpWritten are comparisons and cannot fire without the
            `kinowo_heapdumps_*` series, so a heap dump on this node would land unannounced.
            Either `fleet.heapDumps.enable` is off here or its units were never switched in --
            auto-apply refuses the switch that first introduces them; docs/heap-dumps.md has the
            command. AN ABSENT SERIES IS NOT A NODE WITH NO DUMPS.
